           "extraction_notes": "T1 Two paste mixes at a fixed water-binder ratio of 0.4, GPC and LC3 with 56 percent cement, 30 percent calcined clay and 14 percent limestone, and two EN 196-1 mortar mixes with equivalent binder proportions are extracted; the paper's primary six concrete mixtures across 25, 32 and 45 MPa grades have no home in a schema restricted to paste and mortar system types and were left out entirely. T2 No paste or mortar quantity is measured by more than one route in the extracted scope; EN 196-1 mortar compression and paste nitrogen-adsorption porosimetry are single-route tests. T3 Ambiguities: the mortar water-binder ratio is computable as 0.5 from the Table 5 batch masses but is never stated by the authors for that specific sub-study, so it is left blank rather than derived. T4 Contradictions: none identified between the extracted paste and mortar tables and the surrounding text. T5 No paste or mortar value in the extracted scope is figure-only; the six mortar strength values come directly from Table 5 with printed standard deviations. T6 Three raw-material particle-size-distribution series from Fig. 1 and four hardened-paste pore-size distribution series from Fig. 5 were digitised, all continuous and log-axis readable; the excluded concrete figures were not touched. T7 The BJH nitrogen-adsorption method has no exact vocab_methods entry and is held under the nearest N2-physisorption bucket with a method-level unmapped_terms proposal; concrete-scale shrinkage and empirical model-fitting tables have no representation in a paste/mortar materials schema and were excluded by design, not by oversight. T8 No extracted paste or mortar value exceeds a plausible range; LC3 mortar strengths track slightly below GPC at early age and converge by 28 days, consistent with typical LC3 kinetics. T9 materials_status and mixes_status are complete for the extracted paste/mortar scope; the coverage vector does not attempt to describe the excluded concrete-scale portion of the paper.",
